

ASTROMETRIC PROTON PROPULSION SIMULATION ENGINE (APP‑SE)

Scientific White Sheet — Concept, Physics, Architecture, and Research Intent

Author: C. Matthew Blackwell — Independent Designer, Principal Architect of APP‑SE

1. Purpose & Scope

APP‑SE is a scientific simulation instrument designed to model laser‑driven proton propulsion inside real astrophysical environments.It integrates plasma physics, cosmic‑scale field ingestion, magnetic nozzle dynamics, and HPC execution into a unified engine simulation framework.

APP‑SE is intended for:

Plasma physics research

Astrophysical environment modeling

High‑specific‑impulse propulsion concept studies

Multi‑scale field interaction analysis

2. Scientific Motivation

Traditional propulsion simulations treat engines as isolated systems.APP‑SE challenges that assumption by embedding the engine inside cosmic structure fields, allowing researchers to study:

Proton acceleration under astrophysical boundary conditions

Magnetic field topology effects on thrust

Cosmic‑ray flux modulation of proton energy

Environmental turbulence impacts on beam stability

This enables exploration of propulsion concepts that respond to — and leverage — the physics of the universe itself.

3. Core Physics Model

3.1 Lorentz Force Framework

[ F = q(\mathbf{E} + \mathbf{v} \times \mathbf{B}) ]

This governs proton motion inside laser‑generated and cosmic‑derived fields.

3.2 Proton Acceleration Mechanisms

TNSA — electron sheath acceleration

RPA — radiation pressure acceleration

Hybrid modes — sheath + pressure coupling

3.3 Magnetic Nozzle Dynamics

Magnetic fields shape, collimate, and steer the proton beam:

Divergence control

Thrust vectoring

Beam compression

Efficiency optimization

3.4 Plasma & Field Solvers

APP‑SE uses:

PIC plasma evolution

Boris relativistic particle pusher

Yee lattice Maxwell solver

Collision operator (Coulomb + Bremsstrahlung)

MHD coupling for large‑scale field behavior

